Optional NGINX edge: explicit transport and certificate ownership
===============================================================

Caddy is the default edge. These requirements apply to the optional
edge-nginx service when it replaces Caddy. Do not start both services on the
same published ports.

SSL_MODE=self-signed or SSL_MODE=letsencrypt requires nonempty readable
fullchain.pem and privkey.pem in the read-only /etc/nginx/ssl mount. Missing,
empty or unreadable material now refuses startup; it never falls back to HTTP.
NGINX also validates the certificate/key when loading its TLS configuration.
This intentionally changes the previous unsafe missing-certificate behavior.

Use SSL_MODE=off only as an explicit HTTP deployment, such as a private edge
behind a separately configured TLS-terminating proxy. Protect that internal
link and forwarded-header trust separately. Do not switch to off merely to
make a failed TLS health check green.

The shipped image runs as UID/GID999. The source key must be readable by that
container identity; a read-only bind mount does not grant permissions, and
copying the key to tmpfs cannot bypass source ownership. Deploy a dedicated
runtime copy with restricted owner or group read access. Do not make the key
world-readable or loosen permissions on the original ACME/private-key store.
Account for user-namespace/rootless mappings when choosing host ownership.
The entrypoint leaves the mounted source unchanged and writes its runtime
key copy into tmpfs with mode0600. Renewals require securely updating that
runtime source and reloading/recreating NGINX after validation.

The ACME challenge directory is only a webroot; this container does not obtain
or renew certificates. Use the configured certificate-management process.
For a nonstandard published HTTPS port, use the explicit HTTPS URL and verify
redirect behavior in the surrounding proxy. The TLS template's HTTP redirect
targets the standard HTTPS authority and does not infer Docker port mappings.

/healthz is edge liveness. /health and /health/* are SPA routes. Check both
HTTPS document delivery and the backend /api/v1/health/ready endpoint; a green
edge probe alone does not establish backend readiness or certificate validity
as seen by an external client. Do not use insecure TLS client flags to qualify
the deployment. This document is not evidence of a public-CA, customer proxy,
certificate-renewal or multi-host acceptance test.
